pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% refused-at: parse
% INTEGRATION-TEST: lenient-header-counts
% Regression test for the bound that makes sizing the variable space from the
% file's contents safe. The checker is densely indexed by variable -- the
% variable-name table, the propagation `Assignment` and every watchlist are
% `Vec`s indexed by variable -- so a run costs about 180 bytes and 68 ns per
% index up to the largest one used, whatever the file's size. This 24-byte file
% names variable 4294967300, which is ~682 GiB of tables before the first proof
% line: the process would die in the allocator or to the OOM killer, and neither
% is a verdict.
%
% `Lit::MAX_VAR_IDX` does not catch it (4294967300 is perfectly representable),
% so `MAX_DENSE_VAR_IDX` refuses it as a parse error naming the index. Like the
% representability check, this one is unconditional: the instance carries
% `lenient-header-counts` exactly so that a flag which only relaxes a truncation
% heuristic cannot be mistaken for one that relaxes this.
f 1;
output NONE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
